        "name": "Juno 0.1.11",
        "notes": "# A browser security fix, PDFs signed for you, and updates that never strand you\n\n## What's new\n\n- A security fix for the browser (Chromium 157.0.8103.50). A Chrome bug (CVE-2026-125350) was found being exploited on 10 November, and Juno would not open its browser until it had the fix. This build has it. From now on Juno keeps its browser running for three days while such a fix arrives, tells you at once, and asks before every step it takes for you until you update.\n\n- Sign PDFs: save your signature once (draw it, type it or take a photo of it; it stays on your Mac, encrypted, and never goes to a model or the cloud). When a PDF asks to be signed, Juno finds the place, shows you the page with your signature on it, and signs a copy after one yes from you.\n- Downloads has a home too (Cmd+Shift+J, or Downloads in the menu): one list of what you downloaded and what your chats saved, with a search that looks inside the files (\"from:\", \"type:\", \"in:\", \"added:\", \"size:\") and kinds you can filter by. Juno reads each file on your Mac only, and tells you what a file is, the next step it suggests and which files need you (a paper to sign, an installer, an archive, an exact copy). Tidy up lists installers you already installed, old installers, exact copies and downloads that never finished, each with its reason; nothing is moved, deleted or installed without your yes, and everything it removes goes to the Trash with a 30-day Undo. Downloads made while Juno was closed show up too, from the last 30 days.\n- Agents have a home of their own, beside All chats: the list, each agent's page and history, how it has been running, what started each run and what happens while your Mac sleeps.\n- Agents run on time like a scheduler: a run cut off by a quit is tried once more, a job set for 2:30 AM no longer runs an hour early on the day the clocks change, and a run that is waiting for your yes is never failed by its time limit.\n- Report a problem: one sheet, from a run, a page or the menu bar, that shows what the team will see before you send it. Names, numbers and addresses are taken out unless you choose to include them.\n- The workspace menu in the sidebar is in three quiet groups, and a new chat keeps its row in the list while you look at another tab.\n- Welcome back: a returning person, or a Mac that has been used, is no longer taken through onboarding again.\n- Forms: an email field takes the address the site already knows you by, instead of asking.\n- A page where Juno left a step for you (a held send, a payment, a signature or a question) is kept and handed to you when the run ends.\n\n## Faster and calmer\n\n- Reading a page is faster: a page with thousands of rows reads about 40 percent quicker, a page that never answers says so after 10 seconds, and a crashed tab no longer holds a run for 20 seconds.\n- Juno at rest uses less of your Mac: tabs in the background and covered windows are allowed to rest, reading a page no longer leaves it awake afterwards, and Recall looks at a window only when its picture changed.\n- Screenshots Juno takes for itself are quicker to make, and the text-reading helper stays ready between pages.\n- Windows, cards and buttons keep their words inside them at every size, in the notch, the confirmation sheet, the live view, Settings and the signing card.\n\n## Updates\n\n- Juno is never stranded again: when its browser is too old it offers Update Juno first, warns you from 14 days before, and a required update installs by itself once your Mac has been idle for five minutes with nothing running.\n- Updates now roll out in steps, and can be paused for everyone at once if something is wrong.\n\n## Privacy and safety\n\n- Juno never types, presses or sends anything into a human check, such as a CAPTCHA, by any path. A check stops a run only when it is in the way, not when it sits in some other form on the page.\n\nThe full notes are in What's new after the update.",
